window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2017-06-3", "source": "2017年06月真题·第3套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an advertisement on your campus website to sell a computer you used at college. Your advertisement may include its brand, specifications/features, content, their condition and price, and your contact information. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "Are you looking for an affordable laptop for your studies? I am selling my Dell Inspiron 15, which has served\nme faithfully throughout my four years at college and is now ready for a new owner.\nThe laptop runs on an Intel Core i5 processor with 16 GB of memory and a 512 GB solid-state drive, so it\nhandles coursework, coding and video easily. It has a 15.6-inch full-HD screen and a battery that still lasts six\nhours. Although I have used it since my freshman year, it remains in excellent condition, with no scratches and\na keyboard that works perfectly.\nSince I am graduating soon, I am offering it at only 1,800 yuan, far below its original price, and I will include\nthe charger and a protective case for free.\nIf you are interested, please email me at seller123@campus.edu or add my WeChat (ID: campusdeal). Feel free\nto message me anytime to arrange a viewing. Do not miss this great deal!"}, "translation": {"prompt": "珠江是华南一大河系，流经广州市，是中国第三长的河流，仅次于长江和黄河。珠江三角洲(delta)是中国最发达的地区之一，面积约11,000平方公里。它在面积和人口方面也是世界上最大的城市聚集区。珠江三角洲九个最大城市共有5,700多万人口。上世纪70年代末中国改革开放以来，珠江三角洲已成为中国和世界主要经济区域和制造中心之一。", "sample": "The Pearl River, a major river system in South China which flows through Guangzhou, is the third- longest river in China, after the Yangtze River and the Yellow River. The Pearl River Delta is one of the most developed regions in China, with an area of about 11,000 square kilometers. It is also the largest urban agglomeration in the world in terms of both area and population. The nine largest cities in the Pearl River Delta have a combined population of more than 57 million. Since China's reform and opening-up in the late 1970s, the Pearl River Delta has become one of the major economic regions and manufacturing centers in China and the world."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "America's Internet is faster than ever before, but people still complain about their Internet being too slow.\nNew York's Attorney General's office  __26__  an investigation in the fall into whether or not Verizon, Cablevision and Time Warner are delivering broadband that's as fast as the providers  __27__  it is. Earlier this month, the office asked for the public's help to measure their speed results, saying consumers  __28__  to get the speeds they were promised. \"Too many of us may be paying for one thing, and getting another,\" the Attorney General said.\nIf the investigation uncovers anything, it wouldn't be the first time a telecom provider got into  __29__  over the broadband speeds it promised and delivered customers. Back in June, the Federal Communications Commission fined AT&T $100 million over  __30__  that the carrier secretly reduced wireless speeds after customers consumed a certain amount of  __31__  .\nEven when they stay on the right side of the law, Internet providers arouse customers' anger over bandwidth speed and cost. Just this week, an investigation found that media and telecom giant Comcast is the most  __32__  provider. Over 10 months, Comcast received nearly 12,000 customer complaints, many  __33__  to its monthly data cap and overage(超过额度的) charges.\nSome Americans are getting so  __34__  with Internet providers they're just giving up. A recent study found that the number of Americans with high-speed Internet at home today  __35__  fell during the last two years, and 15% of people now consider themselves to be \"cord-cutters.\"", "matchIntro": "From Accountant to Yogi: Making a Radical Career Change", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "At some point, almost all of us will experience a period of radical professional change. Some of us will seek it out; for others it will feel like an unwelcome intrusion into otherwise stable careers. Either way, we have choices about how we respond to it when it comes."}, {"label": "B", "text": "We recently caught up with yoga entrepreneur Leah Zaccaria, who put herself through the fire of change to completely reinvent herself. In her search to live a life of purpose, Leah left her high-paying accounting job, her husband, and her home. In the process, she built a radically new life and career. Since then, she has founded two yoga studios, met a new life partner, and formed a new community of people. Even if your personal reinvention is less drastic, we think there are lessons from her experience that apply."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Where do the seeds of change come from? The Native American Indians have a saying: \"Pay attention to the whispers so you won't have to hear the screams.\" Often the best ideas for big changes come from unexpected places—it's just a matter of tuning in. Great leaders recognize the weak signals or slight signs that point to big changes to come. Leah reflects on a time she listened to the whispers: \"About the time my daughter was five years old. I started having a sense that 'this isn't right.'\" She then realized that her life no longer matched her vision for it."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Up until that point, Leah had followed traditional measures of success. After graduating with a degree in business and accounting, she joined a public accounting firm, married, bought a house, put lots of stuff in it, and had a baby. \"I did what everybody else thought looked successful,\" she says. Leah easily could have fallen into a trap of feeling content; instead, her energy sparked a period of experimentation and renewal."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Feeling the need to change, Leah started playing with future possibilities by exploring her interests and developing new capabilities. First trying physical exercise and dieting, she lost some weight and discovered an inner strength. \"I felt powerful because I broke through my own limitations,\" she recalls."}, {"label": "F", "text": "However, it was another interest that led Leah to radically reinvent herself. \"I remember sitting on a bench with my aunt at a yoga studio,\" she said, \"and having a moment of clarity right then and there: Yoga is saving my life. Yoga is waking me up. I'm not happy and I want to change and I'm done with this.\" In that moment of clarity Leah made an important leap, conquering her inner resistance to change and making a firm commitment to take bigger steps."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Creating the future you want is a lot easier if you are ready to exploit the opportunities that come your way. When Leah made the commitment to change, she primed herself to new opportunities she may otherwise have overlooked. She recalls:"}, {"label": "H", "text": "One day a man I worked with, Ryan, who had his office next to mine, said, \"Leah, let's go look at this space on Queen Anne.\" He knew my love for yoga and had seen a space close to where he lived that he thought might be good to serve as a yoga studio. As soon as I saw the location, I knew this was it. Of course I was scared, yet I had this strong sense of \"I have to do this.\" Only a few months later Leah opened her first yoga studio, but success was not instant."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Creating the future takes time. That's why leaders continue to manage the present while building toward the big changes of the future. When it's time to make the leap, they take action and immediately drop what's no longer serving their purpose. Initially Leah stayed with her accounting job while starting up the yoga studio to make it all work."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Soon after, she knew she had to make a bold move to fully commit to her new future. Within two years, Leah shed the safety of her accounting job and made the switch complete. Such drastic change is not easy."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Steering through change and facing obstacles brings us face to face with our fears. Leah reflects on one incident that triggered her fears, when her investors threatened to shut her down: \"I was probably up against the most fear I've ever had\" she says. \"I had spent two years cultivating this community, and it had become successful very fast, but within six months I was facing the prospect of losing it all.\""}, {"label": "L", "text": "She connected with her sense of purpose and dug deep, cultivating a tremendous sense of strength. \"I was feeling so intentional and strong that I wasn't going to let fear just take over. I was thinking, 'OK, guys, if you want to try to shut me down, shut me down.' And I knew it was a negotiation scheme, so I was able to say to myself, 'This is not real.'\" By naming her fears and facing them head-on, Leah gained confidence. For most of us, letting go of the safety and security of the past gives us great fear. Calling out our fears explicitly, as Leah did, can help us act decisively."}, {"label": "M", "text": "The cycle of renewal never ends. Leah's growth spurred her to open her second studio—and it wasn't for the money."}, {"label": "N", "text": "I have no desire to make millions of dollars. It's not about that; it's about growth for me. Honestly, I didn't need to open a second studio. I was making as much money as I was as an accountant. But I know if you don't grow, you stand still, and that doesn't work for me."}, {"label": "O", "text": "Consider the current moment in your own life, your team or your organization. Where are you in the cycle of renewal: Are you actively preserving the present, or selectively forgetting the past, or boldly creating the future? What advice would Leah give you to move you ahead on your journey? Once we're on the path of growth, we can continually move through the seasons of transformation and renewal."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Urbanization—migration away from the suburbs to the city center—will be the biggest real estate trend in 2015, according to a new report.\nThe report says America's urbanization will continue to be the most significant issue affecting the industry, as cities across the country imitate the walkability and transit-oriented development making cities like New York and San Francisco so successful.\nAs smaller cities copy the model of these \"24-hour cities,\" more affordable versions of these places will be created. The report refers to this as the coming of the \"18-hour city,\" and uses the term to refer to cities like Houston, Austin, Charlotte, and Nashville, which are \"positioning themselves as highly competitive, in terms of livability, employment offerings, and recreational and cultural facilities.\"\nAnother trend that looks significant in 2015 is that America's largest population group, Millennials(千禧一代), will continue to put off buying a house. Apartments will retain their appeal for a while for Millennials, haunted by what happened to home-owning parents.\nThis trend will continue into the 2020s, the report projects. After that, survey respondents disagree over whether this generation will follow in their parents' footsteps, moving to the suburbs to raise families, or will choose to remain in the city center.\nAnother issue affecting real estate in the coming year will be America's failing infrastructure. Most roads, bridges, transit, water systems, the electric grid, and communications networks were installed 50 to 100 years ago, and they are largely taken for granted until they fail.\nThe report's writers state that America's failure to invest in infrastructure impacts not only the health of the real-estate market, but also our ability to remain globally competitive.\nApart from the specific trends highlighted above, which cause some investors to worry, the report portrays an overall optimism borne by the recent healthy real-estate \"upcycle\" and improving economy. Seventy-four percent of the respondents surveyed report a \"good to excellent\" expectation of real-estate profitability in 2015. While excessive optimism can promote bad investment patterns, resulting in a real-estate \"bubble,\" the report's writers downplay that potential outcome in that it has not yet occurred.", "47": "Urbanization—migration away from the suburbs to the city center—will be the biggest real estate trend in 2015, according to a new report.\nThe report says America's urbanization will continue to be the most significant issue affecting the industry, as cities across the country imitate the walkability and transit-oriented development making cities like New York and San Francisco so successful.\nAs smaller cities copy the model of these \"24-hour cities,\" more affordable versions of these places will be created. The report refers to this as the coming of the \"18-hour city,\" and uses the term to refer to cities like Houston, Austin, Charlotte, and Nashville, which are \"positioning themselves as highly competitive, in terms of livability, employment offerings, and recreational and cultural facilities.\"\nAnother trend that looks significant in 2015 is that America's largest population group, Millennials(千禧一代), will continue to put off buying a house. Apartments will retain their appeal for a while for Millennials, haunted by what happened to home-owning parents.\nThis trend will continue into the 2020s, the report projects. After that, survey respondents disagree over whether this generation will follow in their parents' footsteps, moving to the suburbs to raise families, or will choose to remain in the city center.\nAnother issue affecting real estate in the coming year will be America's failing infrastructure. Most roads, bridges, transit, water systems, the electric grid, and communications networks were installed 50 to 100 years ago, and they are largely taken for granted until they fail.\nThe report's writers state that America's failure to invest in infrastructure impacts not only the health of the real-estate market, but also our ability to remain globally competitive.\nApart from the specific trends highlighted above, which cause some investors to worry, the report portrays an overall optimism borne by the recent healthy real-estate \"upcycle\" and improving economy. Seventy-four percent of the respondents surveyed report a \"good to excellent\" expectation of real-estate profitability in 2015. While excessive optimism can promote bad investment patterns, resulting in a real-estate \"bubble,\" the report's writers downplay that potential outcome in that it has not yet occurred.", "48": "Urbanization—migration away from the suburbs to the city center—will be the biggest real estate trend in 2015, according to a new report.\nThe report says America's urbanization will continue to be the most significant issue affecting the industry, as cities across the country imitate the walkability and transit-oriented development making cities like New York and San Francisco so successful.\nAs smaller cities copy the model of these \"24-hour cities,\" more affordable versions of these places will be created. The report refers to this as the coming of the \"18-hour city,\" and uses the term to refer to cities like Houston, Austin, Charlotte, and Nashville, which are \"positioning themselves as highly competitive, in terms of livability, employment offerings, and recreational and cultural facilities.\"\nAnother trend that looks significant in 2015 is that America's largest population group, Millennials(千禧一代), will continue to put off buying a house. Apartments will retain their appeal for a while for Millennials, haunted by what happened to home-owning parents.\nThis trend will continue into the 2020s, the report projects. After that, survey respondents disagree over whether this generation will follow in their parents' footsteps, moving to the suburbs to raise families, or will choose to remain in the city center.\nAnother issue affecting real estate in the coming year will be America's failing infrastructure. Most roads, bridges, transit, water systems, the electric grid, and communications networks were installed 50 to 100 years ago, and they are largely taken for granted until they fail.\nThe report's writers state that America's failure to invest in infrastructure impacts not only the health of the real-estate market, but also our ability to remain globally competitive.\nApart from the specific trends highlighted above, which cause some investors to worry, the report portrays an overall optimism borne by the recent healthy real-estate \"upcycle\" and improving economy. Seventy-four percent of the respondents surveyed report a \"good to excellent\" expectation of real-estate profitability in 2015. While excessive optimism can promote bad investment patterns, resulting in a real-estate \"bubble,\" the report's writers downplay that potential outcome in that it has not yet occurred.", "49": "Urbanization—migration away from the suburbs to the city center—will be the biggest real estate trend in 2015, according to a new report.\nThe report says America's urbanization will continue to be the most significant issue affecting the industry, as cities across the country imitate the walkability and transit-oriented development making cities like New York and San Francisco so successful.\nAs smaller cities copy the model of these \"24-hour cities,\" more affordable versions of these places will be created. The report refers to this as the coming of the \"18-hour city,\" and uses the term to refer to cities like Houston, Austin, Charlotte, and Nashville, which are \"positioning themselves as highly competitive, in terms of livability, employment offerings, and recreational and cultural facilities.\"\nAnother trend that looks significant in 2015 is that America's largest population group, Millennials(千禧一代), will continue to put off buying a house. Apartments will retain their appeal for a while for Millennials, haunted by what happened to home-owning parents.\nThis trend will continue into the 2020s, the report projects. After that, survey respondents disagree over whether this generation will follow in their parents' footsteps, moving to the suburbs to raise families, or will choose to remain in the city center.\nAnother issue affecting real estate in the coming year will be America's failing infrastructure. Most roads, bridges, transit, water systems, the electric grid, and communications networks were installed 50 to 100 years ago, and they are largely taken for granted until they fail.\nThe report's writers state that America's failure to invest in infrastructure impacts not only the health of the real-estate market, but also our ability to remain globally competitive.\nApart from the specific trends highlighted above, which cause some investors to worry, the report portrays an overall optimism borne by the recent healthy real-estate \"upcycle\" and improving economy. Seventy-four percent of the respondents surveyed report a \"good to excellent\" expectation of real-estate profitability in 2015. While excessive optimism can promote bad investment patterns, resulting in a real-estate \"bubble,\" the report's writers downplay that potential outcome in that it has not yet occurred.", "50": "Urbanization—migration away from the suburbs to the city center—will be the biggest real estate trend in 2015, according to a new report.\nThe report says America's urbanization will continue to be the most significant issue affecting the industry, as cities across the country imitate the walkability and transit-oriented development making cities like New York and San Francisco so successful.\nAs smaller cities copy the model of these \"24-hour cities,\" more affordable versions of these places will be created. The report refers to this as the coming of the \"18-hour city,\" and uses the term to refer to cities like Houston, Austin, Charlotte, and Nashville, which are \"positioning themselves as highly competitive, in terms of livability, employment offerings, and recreational and cultural facilities.\"\nAnother trend that looks significant in 2015 is that America's largest population group, Millennials(千禧一代), will continue to put off buying a house. Apartments will retain their appeal for a while for Millennials, haunted by what happened to home-owning parents.\nThis trend will continue into the 2020s, the report projects. After that, survey respondents disagree over whether this generation will follow in their parents' footsteps, moving to the suburbs to raise families, or will choose to remain in the city center.\nAnother issue affecting real estate in the coming year will be America's failing infrastructure. Most roads, bridges, transit, water systems, the electric grid, and communications networks were installed 50 to 100 years ago, and they are largely taken for granted until they fail.\nThe report's writers state that America's failure to invest in infrastructure impacts not only the health of the real-estate market, but also our ability to remain globally competitive.\nApart from the specific trends highlighted above, which cause some investors to worry, the report portrays an overall optimism borne by the recent healthy real-estate \"upcycle\" and improving economy. Seventy-four percent of the respondents surveyed report a \"good to excellent\" expectation of real-estate profitability in 2015. While excessive optimism can promote bad investment patterns, resulting in a real-estate \"bubble,\" the report's writers downplay that potential outcome in that it has not yet occurred.", "51": "The brain is a seemingly endless library, whose shelves house our most precious memories as well as our lifetime's knowledge. But is there a point where it reaches capacity?\nThe answer is no, because brains are more sophisticated than that. Instead of just crowding in, old information is sometimes pushed out of the brain for new memories to form.\nPrevious behavioural studies have shown that learning new information can lead to forgetting. But in a new study, researchers demonstrated for the first time how this effect occurs in the brain.\nIn daily life, forgetting actually has clear advantages. Imagine, for instance, that you lost your bank card. The new card you receive will come with a new personal identification number (PIN). Each time you remember the new PIN, you gradually forget the old one. This process improves access to relevant information, without old memories interfering.\nAnd most of us may sometimes feel the frustration of having old memories interfere with new, relevant memories. Consider trying to remember where you parked your car in the same car park you were at a week earlier. This type of memory (where you are trying to remember new, but similar information) is particularly vulnerable to interference.\nWhen we acquire new information, the brain automatically tries to incorporate(合并) it within existing information by forming associations. And when we retrieve(检索) information, both the desired and associated but irrelevant information is recalled.\nThe majority of previous research has focused on how we learn and remember new information. But current studies are beginning to place greater emphasis on the conditions under which we forget, as its importance begins to be more appreciated.\nA very small number of people are able to remember almost every detail of their life. While it may sound like an advantage to many, people with this rare condition often find their unusual ability burdensome.\nIn a sense, forgetting is our brain's way of sorting memories, so the most relevant memories are ready for retrieval. Normal forgetting may even be a safety mechanism to ensure our brain doesn't become too full.", "52": "The brain is a seemingly endless library, whose shelves house our most precious memories as well as our lifetime's knowledge. But is there a point where it reaches capacity?\nThe answer is no, because brains are more sophisticated than that. Instead of just crowding in, old information is sometimes pushed out of the brain for new memories to form.\nPrevious behavioural studies have shown that learning new information can lead to forgetting. But in a new study, researchers demonstrated for the first time how this effect occurs in the brain.\nIn daily life, forgetting actually has clear advantages. Imagine, for instance, that you lost your bank card. The new card you receive will come with a new personal identification number (PIN). Each time you remember the new PIN, you gradually forget the old one. This process improves access to relevant information, without old memories interfering.\nAnd most of us may sometimes feel the frustration of having old memories interfere with new, relevant memories. Consider trying to remember where you parked your car in the same car park you were at a week earlier. This type of memory (where you are trying to remember new, but similar information) is particularly vulnerable to interference.\nWhen we acquire new information, the brain automatically tries to incorporate(合并) it within existing information by forming associations. And when we retrieve(检索) information, both the desired and associated but irrelevant information is recalled.\nThe majority of previous research has focused on how we learn and remember new information. But current studies are beginning to place greater emphasis on the conditions under which we forget, as its importance begins to be more appreciated.\nA very small number of people are able to remember almost every detail of their life. While it may sound like an advantage to many, people with this rare condition often find their unusual ability burdensome.\nIn a sense, forgetting is our brain's way of sorting memories, so the most relevant memories are ready for retrieval. Normal forgetting may even be a safety mechanism to ensure our brain doesn't become too full.", "53": "The brain is a seemingly endless library, whose shelves house our most precious memories as well as our lifetime's knowledge. But is there a point where it reaches capacity?\nThe answer is no, because brains are more sophisticated than that. Instead of just crowding in, old information is sometimes pushed out of the brain for new memories to form.\nPrevious behavioural studies have shown that learning new information can lead to forgetting. But in a new study, researchers demonstrated for the first time how this effect occurs in the brain.\nIn daily life, forgetting actually has clear advantages. Imagine, for instance, that you lost your bank card. The new card you receive will come with a new personal identification number (PIN). Each time you remember the new PIN, you gradually forget the old one. This process improves access to relevant information, without old memories interfering.\nAnd most of us may sometimes feel the frustration of having old memories interfere with new, relevant memories. Consider trying to remember where you parked your car in the same car park you were at a week earlier. This type of memory (where you are trying to remember new, but similar information) is particularly vulnerable to interference.\nWhen we acquire new information, the brain automatically tries to incorporate(合并) it within existing information by forming associations. And when we retrieve(检索) information, both the desired and associated but irrelevant information is recalled.\nThe majority of previous research has focused on how we learn and remember new information. But current studies are beginning to place greater emphasis on the conditions under which we forget, as its importance begins to be more appreciated.\nA very small number of people are able to remember almost every detail of their life. While it may sound like an advantage to many, people with this rare condition often find their unusual ability burdensome.\nIn a sense, forgetting is our brain's way of sorting memories, so the most relevant memories are ready for retrieval. Normal forgetting may even be a safety mechanism to ensure our brain doesn't become too full.", "54": "The brain is a seemingly endless library, whose shelves house our most precious memories as well as our lifetime's knowledge. But is there a point where it reaches capacity?\nThe answer is no, because brains are more sophisticated than that. Instead of just crowding in, old information is sometimes pushed out of the brain for new memories to form.\nPrevious behavioural studies have shown that learning new information can lead to forgetting. But in a new study, researchers demonstrated for the first time how this effect occurs in the brain.\nIn daily life, forgetting actually has clear advantages. Imagine, for instance, that you lost your bank card. The new card you receive will come with a new personal identification number (PIN). Each time you remember the new PIN, you gradually forget the old one. This process improves access to relevant information, without old memories interfering.\nAnd most of us may sometimes feel the frustration of having old memories interfere with new, relevant memories. Consider trying to remember where you parked your car in the same car park you were at a week earlier. This type of memory (where you are trying to remember new, but similar information) is particularly vulnerable to interference.\nWhen we acquire new information, the brain automatically tries to incorporate(合并) it within existing information by forming associations. And when we retrieve(检索) information, both the desired and associated but irrelevant information is recalled.\nThe majority of previous research has focused on how we learn and remember new information. But current studies are beginning to place greater emphasis on the conditions under which we forget, as its importance begins to be more appreciated.\nA very small number of people are able to remember almost every detail of their life. While it may sound like an advantage to many, people with this rare condition often find their unusual ability burdensome.\nIn a sense, forgetting is our brain's way of sorting memories, so the most relevant memories are ready for retrieval. Normal forgetting may even be a safety mechanism to ensure our brain doesn't become too full.", "55": "The brain is a seemingly endless library, whose shelves house our most precious memories as well as our lifetime's knowledge. But is there a point where it reaches capacity?\nThe answer is no, because brains are more sophisticated than that. Instead of just crowding in, old information is sometimes pushed out of the brain for new memories to form.\nPrevious behavioural studies have shown that learning new information can lead to forgetting. But in a new study, researchers demonstrated for the first time how this effect occurs in the brain.\nIn daily life, forgetting actually has clear advantages. Imagine, for instance, that you lost your bank card. The new card you receive will come with a new personal identification number (PIN). Each time you remember the new PIN, you gradually forget the old one. This process improves access to relevant information, without old memories interfering.\nAnd most of us may sometimes feel the frustration of having old memories interfere with new, relevant memories. Consider trying to remember where you parked your car in the same car park you were at a week earlier. This type of memory (where you are trying to remember new, but similar information) is particularly vulnerable to interference.\nWhen we acquire new information, the brain automatically tries to incorporate(合并) it within existing information by forming associations. And when we retrieve(检索) information, both the desired and associated but irrelevant information is recalled.\nThe majority of previous research has focused on how we learn and remember new information. But current studies are beginning to place greater emphasis on the conditions under which we forget, as its importance begins to be more appreciated.\nA very small number of people are able to remember almost every detail of their life. While it may sound like an advantage to many, people with this rare condition often find their unusual ability burdensome.\nIn a sense, forgetting is our brain's way of sorting memories, so the most relevant memories are ready for retrieval. Normal forgetting may even be a safety mechanism to ensure our brain doesn't become too full."}, "questions": [{"id": "2017-06-3-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accusations", "B) actually", "C) claim", "D) communicating", "E) complain", "F) data", "G) deserved", "H) frustrated", "I) hated", "J) launched", "K) relating", "L) times", "M) trouble", "N) usually", "O) worried"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) launched\n动词\n【词性槽】句子缺谓语动词\n【依据】launch an investigation 是地道搭配\nlaunch an investigation 意为\"发起一项调查\"，是新闻语体里的高频固定搭配。New\nYork's Attorney General's office __(26)__ an investigation in the fall —— 总检察长办公\n室在秋天发起调查，与下文\"earlier this month, the office asked for the public's help\"形\n成时间链。\n【竞争词】C) claim 形态与搭配双不符\nclaim 原形不能直接作过去时谓语；且 claim an investigation（声称一项调查）搭配不通。C)\nclaim 应留给第 27 空 the providers __ it is 那个一般现在时位置。\nE) complain 原形 + 搭配不通\ncomplain 原形不能作过去式谓语；且 complain an investigation 不成话，complain 通常\n接 about/that。\nG) deserved 语义指向反\ndeserved an investigation（应得一项调查）逻辑上诡异——办公室是\"调查方\"而非\"被调查\n方\"，不可能\"应得\"调查。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accusations", "B) actually", "C) claim", "D) communicating", "E) complain", "F) data", "G) deserved", "H) frustrated", "I) hated", "J) launched", "K) relating", "L) times", "M) trouble", "N) usually", "O) worried"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) claim\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 the providers 后缺谓语\n【依据】运营商\"声称\"网速最贴语境\n全文核心矛盾就是\"运营商声称的网速 vs 实际网速\"。broadband that's as fast as the\nproviders claim it is —— 宽带是否真的有运营商声称的那么快，claim（声称）正是这一\n矛盾的核心动词。后文 the Attorney General 提到 \"Too many of us may be paying for\none thing, and getting another\" 也呼应\"声称与实际不符\"。\n【竞争词】E) complain 主语逻辑反了\ncomplain 意为\"投诉\"，本句主语是 the providers（运营商），运营商不会投诉自家网速；投诉\n的是用户。E) complain 应留给主语为 customers/people 的空（实际上首段 people still\ncomplain 已经把它用掉的可能性都点明了）。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accusations", "B) actually", "C) claim", "D) communicating", "E) complain", "F) data", "G) deserved", "H) frustrated", "I) hated", "J) launched", "K) relating", "L) times", "M) trouble", "N) usually", "O) worried"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) deserved\n动词\n【词性槽】宾语从句主语后缺谓语 + 后接不定式\n【依据】deserve to 完美对位\"理应得到\"\nconsumers deserved to get the speeds they were promised —— 消费者理应得到当初\n被承诺的网速。deserved to + 动词原形是固定结构；语义上\"承诺过的就应得\"完美对位前\n文的\"虚假宣传\"指控。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accusations", "B) actually", "C) claim", "D) communicating", "E) complain", "F) data", "G) deserved", "H) frustrated", "I) hated", "J) launched", "K) relating", "L) times", "M) trouble", "N) usually", "O) worried"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) trouble\n名词\n【词性槽】got into __ 后接名词\n【依据】get into trouble 是死搭配\nget into trouble 是英语里极高频的固定搭配，意为\"惹上麻烦/陷入困境\"。本段说运营商\n因\"承诺的网速与实际不符\"而被罚款（AT&T 被罚 1 亿美元），正是\"陷入麻烦\"。over the\nbroadband speeds 进一步说明\"在网速问题上惹麻烦\"，搭配与语义双双成立。\n【竞争词】A) accusations 搭配不成立\n英语里没有 get into accusations 这种说法；accusations 通常用 face accusations / over\naccusations，且本空所在位置已为 trouble 这一惯用搭配预留。\nL) times 义不通\nget into times（陷入次数/时代）不成话，与上下文\"被罚款\"的负面语境完全无关。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accusations", "B) actually", "C) claim", "D) communicating", "E) complain", "F) data", "G) deserved", "H) frustrated", "I) hated", "J) launched", "K) relating", "L) times", "M) trouble", "N) usually", "O) worried"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) accusations\n名词\n【词性槽】fined …over __ 后必接名词\n【依据】罚款的根据正是\"指控\"\nFCC 罚 AT&T 一亿美元的依据，正是\"AT&T 偷偷限速\"这件\"指控\"。fined AT&T $100\nmillion over accusations that the carrier secretly reduced wireless speeds —— over\naccusations that …（基于这样一项指控：……）是新闻语体的标准用法，that 后内容正是\n该指控的具体内容。复数 accusations 也契合\"针对多项行为\"的语感。\n【竞争词】L) times 不能接同位语 + 语义不沾\nfined …over times 不成话；times 不带 that 同位语从句，且与\"罚款依据\"毫无语义联系。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accusations", "B) actually", "C) claim", "D) communicating", "E) complain", "F) data", "G) deserved", "H) frustrated", "I) hated", "J) launched", "K) relating", "L) times", "M) trouble", "N) usually", "O) worried"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) data\n名词\n【词性槽】a certain amount of 后接不可数名词\n【依据】无线网语境下 data 即\"流量\"\n本句讲 the carrier secretly reduced wireless speeds after customers consumed a\ncertain amount of __(31)__——运营商在用户消耗一定量的什么之后偷偷降速。无线场景\n里\"消耗一定量的 X\"，X 只能是 data（流量）。data 在通信语境里是\"数据/流量\"的固定说\n法，紧扣 wireless speeds、consumed 两个关键词。\n【竞争词】A) accusations 搭配不成立\nconsume accusations（消耗指控）不成话；accusations 是可数复数，前面也不会用 a\ncertain amount of 来修饰。\nL) times 可数复数与 amount 不搭\ntimes 是可数名词复数，与不可数名词专用的 a certain amount of 不搭；表\"次数\"应说 a\ncertain number of times。\nM) trouble 语义对不上\"消耗\"\ntrouble 虽是不可数，但\"consume a certain amount of trouble（消耗一定量的麻烦）\"不\n通；而且 trouble 这个名词位置已经预定给第 29 空的 get into trouble 固定搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accusations", "B) actually", "C) claim", "D) communicating", "E) complain", "F) data", "G) deserved", "H) frustrated", "I) hated", "J) launched", "K) relating", "L) times", "M) trouble", "N) usually", "O) worried"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) hated\n形容词\n【词性槽】the most __ provider 填形容词最高级\n【依据】投诉量最高 → 最被讨厌\nOver 10 months, Comcast received nearly 12,000 customer complaints —— 十个月\n收到近 1.2 万条投诉，定性是\"用户最讨厌的\"运营商。hated 在此构成 the most hated\nprovider（最被讨厌的运营商），与\"投诉量最大\"完全对应。注意主语是 Comcast（运营\n商，被讨厌的对象），所以用被动义的 -ed 形容词 hated。\n【竞争词】H) frustrated 主语角色反了\nfrustrated 描述\"人感到沮丧\"，主语应是 customers/people，不能用来形容\"运营商\"；说\nComcast is the most frustrated provider 等于说\"Comcast 这家运营商最沮丧\"，与上下文逻\n辑不符。H) frustrated 应留给第 34 空（主语是 some Americans）。\nO) worried 主语角色 + 语义双不符\nworried 同样描述\"人担心\"，主语应是人；且\"最担心的运营商\"语义不通——上下文要说的是用\n户的不满，不是运营商的担忧。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 H) —— 误把 frustrated 套给 Comcast。Comcast is the most\n__(32)__ provider 主语是运营商 Comcast，是\"被讨厌的对象\"，应用被动义的 hated；考\n生若只看 frustrated 是\"情绪形容词\"就草率代入，会忽略\"主语是感受者还是被感受者\"这一\n关键判别。防坑：见到 -ed 情绪形容词先问\"主语是人还是物 / 是发出方还是承受方\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accusations", "B) actually", "C) claim", "D) communicating", "E) complain", "F) data", "G) deserved", "H) frustrated", "I) hated", "J) launched", "K) relating", "L) times", "M) trouble", "N) usually", "O) worried"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) relating\n动词\n【词性槽】many __ to its … 现在分词作后置定语\n【依据】relating to 是固定搭配\"涉及/关于\"\nrelating to 是固定搭配，意为\"涉及、与……相关\"；many relating to its monthly data\ncap and overage charges —— 很多投诉与每月流量上限和超额费用有关。语义上把\n12,000 条投诉的具体内容补充清楚，结构与语义双双对位。\n【竞争词】D) communicating 搭配不通 + 主语不对\ncommunicating to（向……传达）通常以人或机构作主语；用 complaints communicating to\nits monthly data cap（投诉向流量上限传达）逻辑不通——投诉不能\"传达给\"一个数据上限。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accusations", "B) actually", "C) claim", "D) communicating", "E) complain", "F) data", "G) deserved", "H) frustrated", "I) hated", "J) launched", "K) relating", "L) times", "M) trouble", "N) usually", "O) worried"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) frustrated\n形容词\n【词性槽】get + so + adj + with 锁形容词\n【依据】\"沮丧到放弃\"语义闭环\nSome Americans are getting so frustrated with Internet providers they're just giving\nup. —— 一些美国人对运营商沮丧到直接放弃。get frustrated with sb/sth 是地道搭配\n（对……感到沮丧）；后半句 they're just giving up（干脆放弃）与\"沮丧\"形成因果闭环。\n同时呼应首段 people still complain about their Internet being too slow 的情绪基调。\n【竞争词】O) worried 程度与后果不匹配\nworried（担心的）程度比 frustrated（沮丧到受挫的）轻，且\"担心\"通常不会直接导致\"放\n弃\"（giving up）；与下文\"剪线\"的激烈行为不在同一情绪层级。此外 get worried about 更常\n见，而非 get worried with。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accusations", "B) actually", "C) claim", "D) communicating", "E) complain", "F) data", "G) deserved", "H) frustrated", "I) hated", "J) launched", "K) relating", "L) times", "M) trouble", "N) usually", "O) worried"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) actually\n副词\n【词性槽】助动词与实义动词之间填副词\n【依据】\"实际上\"对位反预期\nAmerica's Internet is faster than ever before 说网速更快，按常理\"在家用高速网的人数\n应该上升\"；但实情相反——人数\"实际上下降\"。actually 表\"实际上、事实上\"，专门用来引\n出与预期相反的真相，与下文 15% 的人变成\"剪线族\"形成印证。这种\"反差转折\"是 actually\n的典型用法。\n【竞争词】N) usually 频率义与具体时段相冲\nusually 表\"通常、一般情况下\"，描述高频规律性事件；而本句给的是\"过去两年内\"这一**具体时\n段**的事实，不是\"通常情况\"，二者语义不兼容。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 N) —— 误把 usually 当频率副词填入。the number of Americans\nwith high-speed Internet at home today __(35)__ fell during the last two years ——\n位置在动词 fell 前修饰位，N) usually 词性、位置都合，极容易冒出来；但 during the last\ntwo years 是特定时段、且全句要表\"反预期下降\"，应选 B) actually。防坑：选副词时不仅\n判词性，更要判\"是常态规律还是反预期事实\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Readiness to take advantage of new opportunities will make it easier to create one's desired future.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】抓住新机会、创造想要的未来\n【定位】Creating the future you want is a lot easier if you are ready to exploit the opportunities that\ncome your way.\n如果你已准备好利用迎面而来的机会，那么创造你想要的未来会容易得多。\n【改写】Readiness to take advantage of new opportunities ↔\nready to exploit the opportunities that come your way\n同义替换 + 名词化转动词\nwill make it easier to create one's desired future ↔ Creating the future you want is a lot easier\n句式重构 + 同义短语替换\n【辨邻】 I I 段开头给的是 Creating the future takes time（创造未来需要时间），与题干\nReadiness to take advantage of new opportunities 主张的'机会准备度'不是一回\n事，只是同样在谈 creating the future。\nCreating the future takes time. That's why leaders continue to manage the present while building\ntoward the big changes of the future.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "By conventional standards, Leah was a typical successful woman before she changed her career.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】按传统标准是典型成功女性\n【定位】Up until that point, Leah had followed traditional measures of success. After graduating with\na degree in business and accounting, she joined a public accounting firm, married, bought a\nhouse, put lots of stuff in it, and had a baby. \"I did what everybody else thought looked\nsuccessful,\" she says.\n在那之前，Leah 一直遵循着传统意义上的成功标准。她拿到商科会计学位后，进了一家会\n计师事务所，结了婚，买了房，往里添置了很多东西，并生了孩子。她说：'我做了别人眼里\n看起来成功的所有事。'\n【改写】By conventional standards ↔ traditional measures of success\n同义形容词替换\nLeah was a typical successful woman before she changed her career ↔\nI did what everybody else thought looked successful\n自述印证：'做了大众眼里成功的事'即'典型的成功人士'\n【辨邻】 B B 段提到 she has founded two yoga studios, met a new life partner, and formed\na new community of people（她创办了两家瑜伽馆、遇见了新伴侣、建立了新\n社区），这是改变之后的成功，与题干 before she changed her career 的时间正\n相反。\nSince then, she has founded two yoga studios, met a new life partner, and formed a new community\nof people.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Leah gained confidence by laying out her fears and confronting them directly.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】说出并直面恐惧、由此获得自信\n【定位】By naming her fears and facing them head-on, Leah gained confidence.\n通过为恐惧命名、并正面直对它们，Leah 获得了自信。\n【改写】laying out her fears ↔ naming her fears\n同义动词替换：摊开 ↔ 命名\nconfronting them directly ↔ facing them head-on\n同义短语替换：直接对峙 ↔ 正面直面\nLeah gained confidence ↔ Leah gained confidence\n完全一致\n【辨邻】 K K 段讲的是 I was probably up against the most fear I've ever had（我大概面对过\n最大的恐惧），只在描述恐惧的处境与来源，并没有题干所要求的 laying out +\nconfronting + gained confidence 这一动作链条。\nI was probably up against the most fear I've ever had", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "In search of a meaningful life, Leah gave up what she had and set up her own yoga studios.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】为追求有意义生活、放弃所有并开瑜伽馆\n【定位】In her search to live a life of purpose, Leah left her high-paying accounting job, her husband,\nand her home. In the process, she built a radically new life and career. Since then, she has\nfounded two yoga studios, met a new life partner, and formed a new community of people.\n为了追求一种有意义的生活，Leah 离开了她高薪的会计工作、丈夫和家。在这个过程中，\n她建立了一种焕然一新的生活与事业。从那以后，她创办了两家瑜伽馆、遇见了新伴侣、并\n建立了新的社区。\n【改写】In search of a meaningful life ↔ In her search to live a life of purpose\n同义替换：meaningful ↔ of purpose\ngave up what she had ↔ left her high-paying accounting job, her husband, and her home\n概括上位 ↔ 具体清单\nset up her own yoga studios ↔ she has founded two yoga studios\n同义替换：set up ↔ founded\n【辨邻】 F F 段讲的是 conquering her inner resistance to change and making a firm\ncommitment to take bigger steps（克服内心抵抗、坚定承诺迈更大步），是过程中\n的某个细节时刻，与题干要的'放弃所有并开瑜伽馆'这一总览不匹配。\nconquering her inner resistance to change and making a firm commitment to take bigger steps\nJ J 段虽提到 Leah shed the safety of her accounting job and made the switch\ncomplete（舍弃会计工作、完成切换），但只覆盖题干的'放弃'一半，没有 set up\nyoga studios，更没有 in search of a meaningful life 的总览。\nWithin two years, Leah shed the safety of her accounting job and made the switch complete.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Leah's interest in yoga prompted her to make a firm decision to reshape her life.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】对瑜伽的兴趣促成下定决心重塑生活\n【定位】However, it was another interest that led Leah to radically reinvent herself. \"I remember\nsitting on a bench with my aunt at a yoga studio,\" she said, \"and having a moment of clarity\nright then and there: Yoga is saving my life. Yoga is waking me up. I'm not happy and I\nwant to change and I'm done with this.\" In that moment of clarity Leah made an important\nleap, conquering her inner resistance to change and making a firm commitment to take\nbigger steps.\n然而，真正促使 Leah 彻底重新塑造自我的，是另一种兴趣。她说：'我记得当时和姨妈坐在\n一家瑜伽馆的长凳上，那一刻我顿悟：瑜伽在救我的命。瑜伽在唤醒我。我不开心，我想改\n变，我受够了。'在那一刻，Leah 做出了重要的跨越，克服了内心对改变的抵抗，并下定决\n心迈出更大的步子。\n【改写】Leah's interest in yoga ↔ it was another interest that led Leah to radically reinvent herself\n代词指代 + 同义替换\nprompted her to make a firm decision ↔ making a firm commitment to take bigger steps\n同义替换：firm decision ↔ firm commitment\nto reshape her life ↔ radically reinvent herself\n同义替换：reshape ↔ reinvent\n【辨邻】 E E 段讲 First trying physical exercise and dieting, she lost some weight and\ndiscovered an inner strength（先尝试运动节食，瘦了下来并发现了内在力量），\n这里的兴趣是运动节食、结果是发现内在力量，不是题干要的 firm decision to\nreshape life。\nFirst trying physical exercise and dieting, she lost some weight and discovered an inner strength.\nB B 段虽然提到 she has founded two yoga studios（开了两家瑜伽馆），但属于总览\n式呈现'已发生的结果'，没有写明'兴趣触发下定决心'这一因果链条。\nSince then, she has founded two yoga studios, met a new life partner, and formed a new community\nof people.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Small signs may indicate great changes to come and therefore merit attention.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】微小迹象预示大变化、值得关注\n【定位】Great leaders recognize the weak signals or slight signs that point to big changes to come.\n出色的领导者会留意那些预示着即将到来的重大变化的微弱信号或细小迹象。\n【改写】Small signs may indicate great changes to come ↔\nthe weak signals or slight signs that point to big changes to come\n形容词同义替换 + 动词同义替换\ntherefore merit attention ↔\nPay attention to the whispers so you won't have to hear the screams\nmerit attention ↔ pay attention", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Leah's first yoga studio was by no means an immediate success.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】第一家瑜伽馆绝非立竿见影地成功\n【定位】Only a few months later Leah opened her first yoga studio, but success was not instant.\n仅仅几个月之后，Leah 就开了她的第一家瑜伽馆，但成功并非立竿见影。\n【改写】Leah's first yoga studio ↔ Leah opened her first yoga studio\n完全一致\nwas by no means an immediate success ↔ success was not instant\n强否定 + 同义形容词替换\n【辨邻】 M M 段讲 Leah's growth spurred her to open her second studio（成长推动她开了第\n二家瑜伽馆），是第二家、且没有 by no means immediate success 的描述。\nLeah's growth spurred her to open her second studio—and it wasn't for the money.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Some people regard professional change as an unpleasant experience that disturbs their stable careers.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】有人把职业变化视为打扰稳定职业的不愉快经历\n【定位】Some of us will seek it out; for others it will feel like an unwelcome intrusion into otherwise\nstable careers.\n我们中有一些人会主动追求它；而对另一些人来说，它就像是一种闯入，打扰了原本稳定的\n职业生涯，并不受欢迎。\n【改写】Some people regard professional change as an unpleasant experience ↔\nfor others it will feel like an unwelcome intrusion\n态度动词同义：regard as ↔ feel like；形容词同义：unpleasant ↔ unwelcome\nthat disturbs their stable careers ↔ intrusion into otherwise stable careers\n动词 ↔ 名词化的转换", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "The worst fear Leah ever had was the prospect of losing her yoga business.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】她有过的最大恐惧是可能失去瑜伽事业\n【定位】Leah reflects on one incident that triggered her fears, when her investors threatened to shut\nher down: \"I was probably up against the most fear I've ever had\" she says. \"I had spent two\nyears cultivating this community, and it had become successful very fast, but within six\nmonths I was facing the prospect of losing it all.\"\nLeah 回忆起一次触发她恐惧的事件——当时她的投资人威胁要把她关掉：'我大概面对过这\n一生中最大的恐惧'，她说，'我花了两年时间培育起这个社群，它很快就大获成功，但六个\n月内我就面临着失去这一切的前景。'\n【改写】The worst fear Leah ever had ↔ I was probably up against the most fear I've ever had\n最高级同义：worst ↔ most\nthe prospect of losing her yoga business ↔\nwithin six months I was facing the prospect of losing it all\n代词指代还原 + 关键词 prospect 完全一致\n【辨邻】 L L 段讲的是 By naming her fears and facing them head-on, Leah gained\nconfidence（通过命名和直面恐惧而获得自信），是处理层面，而题干问的是'最大恐\n惧是什么'——前者是后者的下文，不能反过来当源头段。\nBy naming her fears and facing them head-on, Leah gained confidence.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "As she explored new interests and developed new potentials, Leah felt powerful internally.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】探索新兴趣、开发新潜能时感到内在强大\n【定位】Feeling the need to change, Leah started playing with future possibilities by exploring her\ninterests and developing new capabilities. First trying physical exercise and dieting, she lost\nsome weight and discovered an inner strength. \"I felt powerful because I broke through my\nown limitations,\" she recalls.\n感到需要改变，Leah 开始通过探索自己的兴趣、开发新的能力，去尝试未来的各种可能。\n她先是尝试运动和节食，瘦了一些，并发现了一种内在的力量。她回忆道：'我感觉很强大，\n因为我突破了自己的极限。'\n【改写】she explored new interests ↔ exploring her interests\n完全同义动词\nand developed new potentials ↔ developing new capabilities\n同义名词替换：potentials ↔ capabilities\nLeah felt powerful internally ↔ I felt powerful because I broke through my own limitations\n副词补足语 internally ↔ 突破内在极限的因果\n【辨邻】 F F 段虽然也涉及兴趣（瑜伽），但聚焦于 having a moment of clarity right then\nand there（顿悟）与下定决心，没有 felt powerful 这一情绪表述。\nhaving a moment of clarity right then and there", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "According to the new report, real estate development in 2015 will witness_______.", "options": ["A) An accelerating speed", "B) A shift to city centers", "C) A new focus on small cities", "D) An ever-increasing demand"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】will witness 是要求从原文找到某条与 2015 年地产趋势直接对应的客观陈述，不要求做逻\n辑延伸。\n【拆句】According to the new report, real estate development in 2015 will witness_______. 是\n一个待补全的陈述句，主干是 real estate development \\.\\.\\. will witness，witness 这里\n是「见证、出现」的熟词僻义，主语是事件而非人，相当于 will see。横线上要填的是被见\n证的对象（一个名词短语）。\n【定位】Urbanization—migration away from the suburbs to the city center—will be the biggest real\nestate trend in 2015, according to a new report.\n根据一份新报告，城市化——即人口从郊区迁回市中心——将是 2015 年地产业最大的\n趋势。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nan accelerating speed 在 P1 中没有任何对应——原文只说趋势是什么，没说趋势在\n加速。\nB) ✓ 正确\nmigration away from the suburbs to the city center ↔ a shift to city centers\n四个选项中只有 a shift to city centers 完整覆盖了定位句的核心语义「迁向市中心」，\n其他三项均与定位句无对应。\nC) 张冠李戴\na new focus on small cities 取自 smaller cities 那一段，但那是后文对 18 小时城市的\n延伸描述，不是 P1 概括的 2015 年首要趋势。\nD) 无中生有\nan ever-increasing demand 在 P1 完全找不到对应——原文讲的是地理迁移方向，不\n是需求量的变化。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What characterizes \"24-hour cities\" like New York?", "options": ["A) People can live without private cars.", "B) People are generally more competitive.", "C) People can enjoy services around the clock.", "D) People are in harmony with the environment."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What characterizes \"24-hour cities\" 问的是该类城市的客观特征，要从原文找出对 24-\nhour cities 的属性描述。\n【拆句】What characterizes \"24-hour cities\" like New York? 是 What 作主语的特殊疑问句，主\n干是 What characterizes \"24-hour cities\"，like New York 是举例修饰，可还原为 What\nare the characteristics of 24-hour cities such as New York，无陷阱。\n【定位】America's urbanization will continue to be the most significant issue affecting the industry, as\ncities across the country imitate the walkability and transit-oriented development making\ncities like New York and San Francisco so successful.\n美国的城市化将继续是影响这个行业最重要的议题，因为全国各地的城市都在模仿那种让纽\n约、旧金山等城市如此成功的步行友好和公交导向开发模式。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nPeople can live without private cars\\. 虽然可由 walkability and transit-oriented\ndevelopment 间接推出（能步行、有公交所以不需要车），但原文从未直接说人们「不\n开私家车」，且本题问 characterizes（直接特征），不是问推断。\nB) 张冠李戴\nPeople are generally more competitive\\. 借用了 highly competitive，但 P3 描述的\n是 18 小时城市 positioning themselves as highly competitive（城市的竞争力），不\n是「24 小时城市的居民更有竞争力」。\nC) ✓ 正确\nwalkability and transit-oriented development ↔ People can enjoy services around the clock\\.\n题干引号词 \"24-hour cities\" 自身就告诉你这类城市最大的特征是「24 小时」——能让\n人 around the clock（一天 24 小时）享受服务，enjoy services around the clock 是\n对该概念名最直接的同义解释。\nD) 无中生有\nPeople are in harmony with the environment\\. 在原文中没有任何对应——文章谈的\n是步行和公交，没有讨论人与环境的和谐关系。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "Why are Millennials reluctant to buy a house?", "options": ["A) They can only afford small apartments.", "B) The house prices are currently too high.", "C) Their parents' bad experience still haunts them.", "D) They feel attached to the suburban environment."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】Why are Millennials reluctant to buy a house? 问原因，答案通常是原文中与该事实并列\n出现的另一句因果说明。\n【拆句】Why are Millennials reluctant to buy a house? 是 Why 引导的特殊疑问句，be\nreluctant to do 是固定搭配，意为「不愿做某事」，相当于 not willing to。基础薄弱考生\n最易把 reluctant 当生词卡住，记住它＝unwilling 即可。\n【定位】Apartments will retain their appeal for a while for Millennials, haunted by what happened to\nhome-owning parents.\n公寓在一段时间内对千禧一代仍有吸引力，因为他们仍被自己有房一族父母的遭遇所困扰。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nThey can only afford small apartments\\. 借用了 Apartments 这个词制造熟悉感，但\n原文只说公寓对他们有吸引力，从未说他们「只买得起小公寓」。\nB) 无中生有\nThe house prices are currently too high\\. 完全没有原文依据——文章未提房价，只\n提「迁移趋势」和「父母阴影」。\nC) ✓ 正确\nhaunted by what happened to home-owning parents ↔\nTheir parents' bad experience still haunts them\\.\nC 同时把 haunt 这个动词和「父母遭遇」这个名词概念都做了同义改写，与定位句精确\n对应。\nD) 与原文相反\nThey feel attached to the suburban environment\\. 与全文趋势相反——migration\naway from the suburbs to the city center 明确说人们正在离开郊区进城，而非依恋\n郊区。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What might hinder real estate development in the U.S.?", "options": ["A) The continuing economic recession in the country.", "B) The lack of confidence on the part of investors.", "C) The fierce global competition.", "D) The worsening infrastructure."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What might hinder real estate development 问的是哪个具体事物会阻碍地产发展，原文\n必有一句直接点名该事物。\n【拆句】What might hinder real estate development in the U\\.S\\.? 主干是 What might hinder\nreal estate development，hinder ＝「阻碍、妨碍」，might 是情态动词表可能性，整体\n可还原为「什么可能会阻碍美国地产发展」，无陷阱。\n【定位】Another issue affecting real estate in the coming year will be America's failing infrastructure.\n另一个会影响未来一年地产业的问题是美国正在恶化的基础设施。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nThe continuing economic recession in the country\\. 与 improving economy（经济\n正在改善）直接矛盾——文章基调是经济在好转，没说有 economic recession。\nB) 与原文相反\nThe lack of confidence on the part of investors\\. 与 Seventy-four percent of the\nrespondents surveyed report a \"good to excellent\" expectation 矛盾——74% 的\n人持乐观预期，投资者并未缺乏信心。\nC) 偷换概念\nThe fierce global competition\\. 借用 globally competitive 制造熟悉感，但 P7 说的\n是「基础设施失修削弱我们 remain globally competitive 的能力」，全球竞争是被影\n响的对象，不是 hinder 地产的原因。\nD) ✓ 正确\nAmerica's failing infrastructure ↔ The worsening infrastructure\\.\nD 把 failing 同义替换为 worsening，名词 infrastructure 原词复现，是定位句最精准\n的同义改写。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "How do most of the respondents in the survey feel about the U.S. real-estate market in 2015?", "options": ["A) Pessimistic.", "B) Hopeful.", "C) Cautious.", "D) Uncertain."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度题）\n【判型】How do most of the respondents \\.\\.\\. feel about 直接问态度，要锁定原文里反映被调\n查者整体情绪的描述句。\n【拆句】How do most of the respondents in the survey feel about the U\\.S\\. real-estate\nmarket in 2015? 是 How 引导的特殊疑问句，feel about 意为「对……感觉如何」，in the\nsurvey 修饰 respondents，in 2015 修饰 real-estate market，结构清晰。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nPessimistic\\. 是负面态度，与 overall optimism 极性完全相反。\nB) ✓ 正确\noverall optimism ↔ Hopeful\\.\n\"good to excellent\" expectation of real-estate profitability ↔ Hopeful\\.\nHopeful\\. 既匹配 overall optimism 的极性，又吻合 Seventy-four percent 多数比\n例，是唯一同时覆盖「正面态度＋多数」的选项。\nC) 以偏概全\nCautious\\. 借用 \"bubble\" 风险提示制造合理感，但作者立刻 downplay that potential\noutcome（淡化该风险），表明谨慎是少数声音，不是 most of the respondents 的主\n流情绪。\nD) 与原文相反\nUncertain\\. 意为「不确定的」，与 Seventy-four percent 这一明确的多数比例和\n\"good to excellent\" expectation 这一明确预期相矛盾——被调查者对未来有清晰的正\n面看法，并非不确定。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "态度题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What have past behavioural studies found about our brain?", "options": ["A) Its capacity actually knows no limits.", "B) It grows sophisticated with practice.", "C) It keeps our most precious memories until life's end.", "D) New information learned pushes old information out."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What have past behavioural studies found about our brain? 直问研究的具体发现，句\n中没有 infer/suggest/imply 等推断信号，也不指向全文，属典型细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What have past behavioural studies found about our brain? 主干是 What have\nstudies found about our brain，使用现在完成时强调研究已得出的结论；about our\nbrain 是介词短语修饰 found，指出研究的对象。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Previous behavioural studies have shown that learning new information can lead to forgetting.\n过往的行为学研究已经表明：学习新信息会导致遗忘。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nIts capacity actually knows no limits. 称大脑容量无极限，但定位句只谈过往研究的发\n现是学习引发遗忘，与容量无关；且 Normal forgetting may even be a safety\nmechanism to ensure our brain doesn't become too full 反向暗示大脑确有过载风\n险，故选项与文意相反。\nB) 无中生有\nIt grows sophisticated with practice. 称大脑因练习变得复杂；原文 brains are more\nsophisticated than that 中 sophisticated 是说大脑结构本就复杂，不涉及任何\npractice，选项凭空加入练习这一因果。\nC) 偷换概念\nIt keeps our most precious memories until life's end. 来自 whose shelves house\nour most precious memories，但原文只是开篇用图书馆比喻引出话题，并未声称大脑\n一直保留到生命终点，更不是过往行为研究的发现；且 P2 紧接着否定了大脑容量无限\n的隐含，与本选项矛盾。\nD) ✓ 正确\nlearning new information can lead to forgetting ↔\nNew information learned pushes old information out.\nNew information learned pushes old information out. 把预判中的新信息→遗忘\n旧信息精准还原，且与 P2 的 pushed out 表述呼应，是定位句的同义改写，故为正确\n答案。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What is the benefit of forgetting?", "options": ["A) It frees us from painful memories.", "B) It helps slow down our aging process.", "C) It facilitates our access to relevant information.", "D) It prevents old information from forming associations."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What is the benefit of forgetting? 问的是文中明示的好处，没有 imply、suggest 等推断\n信号，是细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What is the benefit of forgetting? 主干 What is the benefit；of forgetting 是介词\n短语修饰 benefit，forgetting 是动名词，整句结构简单，无陷阱。\n【定位】This process improves access to relevant information, without old memories interfering.\n这一过程改善了对相关信息的获取，使旧记忆不再干扰。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nIt frees us from painful memories. 把 forgetting 的好处扯到摆脱痛苦记忆，但 P4 全\n段谈的是无关旧信息干扰新信息（如旧密码干扰新密码），从未涉及 painful\nmemories，属凭空捏造。\nB) 无中生有\nIt helps slow down our aging process. 把好处定位为延缓衰老，P4 与全文均未提及\naging process，属彻底偏离文本的常识联想。\nC) ✓ 正确\nThis process improves access to relevant information ↔\nIt facilitates our access to relevant information.\nIt facilitates our access to relevant information. 与预判更容易取用相关信息逐词对\n齐，且对应原文同句同结构，是定位句的精准同义改写。\nD) 与原文相反\nIt prevents old information from forming associations. 说遗忘阻止旧信息形成联\n想；但 When we acquire new information, the brain automatically tries to\nincorporate(合并) it within existing information by forming associations. 明示大脑\n形成联想是自动机制，且本段讨论的是遗忘让旧信息不再干扰检索，并非阻止联想形\n成，逻辑相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What is the emphasis of current studies of memory?", "options": ["A) When people tend to forget.", "B) What contributes to forgetting.", "C) How new technology hinders memory capacity.", "D) Why learning and forgetting are complementary."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What is the emphasis of current studies of memory? 直问当前研究的重点，无推断信号\n词，属于细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What is the emphasis of current studies of memory? 主干 What is the\nemphasis；of current studies of memory 是两个 of 介词短语依次修饰 emphasis，逐层\n缩窄到 memory 这一研究领域，无陷阱。\n【定位】But current studies are beginning to place greater emphasis on the conditions under which we\nforget, as its importance begins to be more appreciated.\n但当前的研究开始更加重视我们在何种条件下会遗忘，因为遗忘的重要性正得到越来越多的\n认可。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthe conditions under which we forget ↔ When people tend to forget.\nWhen people tend to forget. 把 conditions under which we forget 抽象为 when\n的疑问从句，与预判遗忘发生的条件精准对应，是正确答案。\nB) 偷换概念\nWhat contributes to forgetting. 看似与 A 仅一字之差，但 contributes to 强调的是\n原因或贡献因素（为什么会遗忘），与 the conditions under which we forget 强调的\n何种条件下（即 when/where 的情境）并非同义；研究遗忘的条件，并不等同于研究遗\n忘的成因。这是四级最经典的差一点干扰项。\nC) 无中生有\nHow new technology hinders memory capacity. 把当前研究的方向扯到新技术对记\n忆的妨碍；全文无任何 technology 出现，属彻底无据。\nD) 过度推断\nWhy learning and forgetting are complementary. 谈学习与遗忘的互补关系；P7 只\n说 majority of previous research has focused on learning，current studies 转向\nforgetting 的条件，并未做出二者互补的归纳。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What do people find about their rare ability to remember every detail of their life?", "options": ["A) It adds to the burden of their memory.", "B) It makes their life more complicated.", "C) It contributes to their success in life.", "D) It constitutes a rare object of envy."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do people find about their rare ability to remember every detail of their life?\n询问拥有特殊能力的人对其感受的客观陈述，无推断信号词，属细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What do people find about their rare ability to remember every detail of their\nlife? 主干 What do people find；about their rare ability 是介词短语指明发现的对象；\nto remember every detail of their life 是不定式作 ability 的后置定语，把 ability 具体化\n为记住一生每个细节。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】While it may sound like an advantage to many, people with this rare condition often find their\nunusual ability burdensome.\n虽然这对许多人来说听起来像是种优势，但拥有这种罕见状态的人往往觉得自己异常的能力\n是个负担。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\noften find their unusual ability burdensome ↔ It adds to the burden of their memory.\nIt adds to the burden of their memory. 用 adds to the burden of their memory 还\n原 burdensome 的负担含义，与预判觉得这种能力是负担一致，是正确答案。\nB) 过度推断\nIt makes their life more complicated. 把负担感扩张到使生活变复杂；原文只说\nunusual ability burdensome，并未谈及 life 的复杂程度，且 burden 与 complicate\n不等义。\nC) 与原文相反\nIt contributes to their success in life. 称这种能力有助于成功；原文明示这种能力是\nburdensome（负担），与有助于成功方向相反。\nD) 过度推断\nIt constitutes a rare object of envy. 称这种能力是令人羡慕的稀有对象；原文 it may\nsound like an advantage to many 只说听起来像优势，未及 envy（羡慕）；且该让步\n从句的核心仍是主句的 burdensome。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the passage say about forgetting?", "options": ["A) It can enlarge our brain capacity.", "B) It helps get rid of negative memories.", "C) It is a way of organising our memories.", "D) It should not cause any alarm in any way."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （主旨题）\n【判型】What does the passage say about forgetting? 中 the passage say about 指向全文性表\n述，而非某段细节，需要把全文关于 forgetting 的论述串成主线后作答。\n【拆句】题干 What does the passage say about forgetting? 主干 What does the passage\nsay；about forgetting 是介词短语修饰 say，指明文章谈论的对象。结构简洁，无陷阱。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nIt can enlarge our brain capacity. 称遗忘能扩大大脑容量；原文 brains are more\nsophisticated than that 已否定容量的简单加减观，Normal forgetting may even be\na safety mechanism to ensure our brain doesn't become too full 也只说防止过\n载，并未说扩大容量。\nB) 无中生有\nIt helps get rid of negative memories. 把 forgetting 的功能定为摆脱负面记忆；全文\n谈的是无关或干扰性记忆（如旧密码、过期车位），从未谈 negative memories，且\nQ52 也已显示原文是 relevant 与 irrelevant 之分，不是 positive 与 negative 之分。\nC) ✓ 正确\nforgetting is our brain's way of sorting memories ↔ It is a way of organising our memories.\nNormal forgetting may even be a safety mechanism to ensure our brain doesn't become too full.\n↔ It is a way of organising our memories.\nIt is a way of organising our memories. 与 forgetting is our brain's way of sorting\nmemories 近乎逐词等值，且覆盖全文论述（好处、机制、当前研究方向都服务于此结\n论），是主旨题正确答案。\nD) 以偏概全\nIt should not cause any alarm in any way. 用两个 any 的绝对化表述称遗忘任何情形\n下都不应引起警觉；原文 And most of us may sometimes feel the frustration of\nhaving old memories interfere with new, relevant memories. 显示有时遗忘确会带\n来困扰，且 P8 提到能记住一切的人觉得是负担，说明记忆相关问题并非毫无值得警惕\n之处，绝对化表述不成立。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2017-06-1"});
